1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,752
The Englund gambit gives up the d pawn for immediate queen activity.

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,365
White takes, and Black develops with tempo.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,977
The queen attacks e five and refuses to retreat quietly.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:18,590
White develops the bishop instead of defending the pawn.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,203
Black checks from b four and makes the queen a target.

6
00:00:23,363 --> 00:00:27,815
The bishop blocks, but Black captures on b two.

7
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:32,428
The queen has won a pawn, yet it is now far from safety.

8
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:37,040
White develops the knight and starts gaining tempi.

9
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,652
Black jumps to b four to keep the initiative alive.

10
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:46,265
The knight reroutes and the c pawn hits the center.

11
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,877
The lesson: a queen can win material and still lose the game.

12
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:55,650
Would you accept the Englund gambit?

